From 9311156fa58543c05df4ab220a7ec176588549fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 01:20:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Open prepared handoffs on Unix and Windows, with a Windows
 held-handle store

The gate's original reason was missing atomic no-follow snapshot-index
publication. The V2 delta implemented it on Unix; this adds the Windows
counterpart, so the gate opens on both and stays closed elsewhere, still with
no configuration or environment-variable bypass.

Windows store (exec-server/src/no_follow/atomic_directory_windows.rs):
- the directory is opened one component at a time, each relative to the
  previous handle with OBJ_DONT_REPARSE, so no component can be a junction,
  symlink or other reparse point; only the volume root is resolved by name
- creates, bounded reads and the final rename are relative to the held handle
- replacement is NtSetInformationFile(FileRenameInformationEx) with
  REPLACE_IF_EXISTS | POSIX_SEMANTICS; kernel32's SetFileInformationByHandle
  rejects a non-null RootDirectory with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER
- a failed directory flush reports VisibleButDurabilityUnconfirmed, as on Unix

Local disk paths in the existing Windows no-follow helpers now use the same
component walk. Resolving a whole \??\X: path with OBJ_DONT_REPARSE fails on
the drive-letter link itself, so those helpers could not open any real file.
UNC paths keep the original single-shot open, which is what keeps
\host\pipe\... from connecting.

Tests: the ten prepare-dependent tests now run on Unix and Windows. Running
them exposed two failures that also occur on Linux: blank handoff text created
the handoff directory before being rejected (now checked first), and the
deny-read test set its policy in a way that never started a turn (now set like
upstream deny-read tests, skipped on Windows like them). Tool and reminder
text explain prepare instead of forbidding it.
